      "header": "Panel A — Megaloblastic Anaemia",
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>The diagnostic macrocytic pattern. <strong>Macro-ovalocytes</strong> — large <em>oval</em> RBCs (clearly bigger than a small-lymphocyte nucleus) — plus a <strong>hypersegmented neutrophil</strong> (≥5 lobes; a 6-lobed cell is the earliest and most specific peripheral clue). Reflects impaired DNA synthesis from B12/folate deficiency (nuclear–cytoplasmic asynchrony).</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "5f906a78-9756-4b06-a629-6aeca612fcf0",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Panel A — Megaloblastic Anaemia"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "position": {
        "x": 75,
        "y": 32,
        "legacyPositioning": false
      },
      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "hotspotIconType": "icon",
      "hotspotIcon": "plus",
      "hotspotColor": "#a4154b",
      "header": "Panel B — Alcohol / Liver Disease",
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Non-megaloblastic</strong> macrocytosis. The large cells are <strong>ROUND</strong> (not oval) and there is <strong>NO hypersegmentation</strong>. <strong>Target cells</strong> are common (excess membrane lipid in liver disease). MCV is usually only mildly raised — contrast with the very high MCV of megaloblastic anaemia.</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "3308b597-ea0c-48c9-96c3-a37f0d1b29f7",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Panel B — Alcohol / Liver Disease"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "position": {
        "x": 25,
        "y": 72,
        "legacyPositioning": false
      },
      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "hotspotIconType": "icon",
      "hotspotIcon": "plus",
      "hotspotColor": "#a4154b",
      "header": "Panel C — Reticulocytosis",
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>A <strong>spurious</strong> macrocytosis: young red cells (reticulocytes) are larger and stain bluish-grey — <strong>polychromasia</strong>. Seen after haemorrhage or haemolysis. The blue-grey tint and a high reticulocyte count distinguish it from true megaloblastic macrocytosis (which has a LOW reticulocyte count).</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "d124d0c9-01d0-4bad-bb4c-03592cb49805",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Panel C — Reticulocytosis"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "position": {
        "x": 75,
        "y": 72,
        "legacyPositioning": false
      },
      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "hotspotIconType": "icon",
      "hotspotIcon": "plus",
      "hotspotColor": "#a4154b",
      "header": "Panel D — Normal (reference)",
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>Reference field: <strong>normocytic, normochromic</strong> RBCs (diameter ≈ a small lymphocyte nucleus, central pallor ≤ ⅓). Use this as the size/colour baseline against which to judge the macrocytes in Panels A–C.</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "ea0320c4-e8ab-400b-a5d4-b5fc28c34b58",
          "metadata": {
